    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you compare the thermal dissipation rates and heat soak characteristics of carbon-ceramic vs. semi-metallic brake pads during repeated high-load deceleration cycles? I'm specifically looking at how the thermal conductivity of the friction material impacts the transfer of heat to the calipers.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Carbon-ceramic brake pads generally exhibit higher thermal dissipation rates and lower heat soak characteristics compared to semi-metallic brake pads, particularly during high-load deceleration cycles. This is due to the higher thermal conductivity (up to 10 W/m-K) of the ceramic material, facilitating efficient heat transfer from the friction surface to the caliper. Semi-metallic brake pads, on the other hand, have significantly lower thermal conductivity (about 1 W/m-K), leading to greater heat accumulation and increased caliper temperatures. As a result, carbon-ceramic pads tend to radiate heat more quickly, reducing brake fade and caliper temperatures.",
      "source": "target",
